Chapter One — The Bridge

The bridge began in fog and ended in nothing he could see.

Dusty stood at the mainland foot of it with a canvas satchel over one shoulder and everything he owned inside it, and he counted. Nine guards at the near gate. Two on the roof of the gatehouse where the crenellations broke the skyline. Spears taller than the men holding them, torso plate polished to a hard morning sheen, shields slung on their backs like the shells of enormous beetles. The nearest one had a chip out of the left greave and had not bothered to have it hammered out. That meant it was recent. That meant the Guard had been busy this week.

Behind him, Aethres was waking. Fifty thousand people making noise about it — cart wheels on wet stone, a fishmonger shouting a price, someone's shutters banging open three streets back. Ahead of him the world simply stopped. White water, white air, and a span of pale stone running out into it, wide enough for six carriages abreast and empty enough to swallow the sound of his own boots.

He had said goodbye an hour ago in a narrow room with clean sheets, which was still the strangest part of all of it. Clean sheets. Rinnie sitting up straighter than her body wanted, chin lifted, the reassuring smile already assembled and waiting for him when he turned around at the door.

*Go on,* she had said. *You're going to be late and then they'll write it down.*

She had pressed something into his palm at the last moment, folded in a scrap of waxed paper. He had not looked at it yet. He could feel the shape of it now against his ribs through the satchel strap, flat and light and weighing more than the rest of the bag.

A horn sounded somewhere up the line. Dusty stepped onto the bridge.

—

The carriages came behind him, and the carriages stopped.

He heard it happen twenty paces back — the shift in the rhythm, hooves cut short, a driver swearing, the particular silence of a great many well-dressed people discovering that a rule applies to them. Dusty did not turn around. He had learned young that turning around invited invitation, and invitation from that class of person had a price attached that never appeared on the receipt.

He heard it anyway. He heard everything.

"—do you have any conception of whose seal that is—"

"The bridge is walked alone, my lord." The guard's voice was flat as a coin. Bored, even. A man reciting a line he had said forty times since dawn and would say sixty more before dark. "Trunks go with the porters. Attendants remain in Aethres. You are welcome to walk."

There was a pause with a great deal of expensive breathing in it.

Then, eventually, footsteps.

Dusty allowed himself a single glance sideways as the young man overtook him — because you always looked, because knowing which shapes in a room could hurt you was not curiosity, it was maintenance. Crimson doublet. Gold thread at the cuffs worth more than the building Dusty had slept in for the first fifteen years of his life. A jaw clenched so hard the muscle stood out like a knot in rope, and hands opening and closing at his sides, over and over, searching for something to be angry at that would not cost him anything.

The young lord looked at him. Took in the patched coat, the boots resoled twice, the satchel.

Whatever conclusion he came to, he came to it fast, and it made him feel better. He walked on with his shoulders back.

Dusty kept his pace exactly the same. It cost him nothing to be measured wrong, and it never had. He watched the man's back diminish into the white ahead, and thought only that he had a long stride and no callus on his right palm worth speaking of, and then he stopped thinking about him at all.

The fog thinned at the center of the span. He had time to see it happen — the white going gauzy, then gold, then tearing open all at once like a curtain pulled off a window.

Mythrandis stood in the water.

The towers came first, because the towers were meant to. Three of them at the island's heart, pale as bone and taller than anything he had ever seen made by hands, catching the low sun on their eastern faces and throwing the light back so hard that he had to narrow his eyes against it. Below and around them, greenery. Not the exhausted green of the Greys, where a weed in a gutter was an event. Deep green, absurd green, forest and garden and terraced hedge spilling down toward a shore that glittered with something he suspected was marble because there was no reason on earth for it to be marble.

The lake lay flat and silver around all of it for miles in every direction, and the far shores were only a smudge, and there was no smoke. That was the thing that stopped him. Fifty square miles of buildings and five thousand people and not one column of chimney smoke anywhere in the sky.

Dusty stood in the middle of the bridge a moment longer than he needed to. Not from wonder. He was pricing it — the walls, the guard rotation, the sight lines from those towers, the distance to that shoreline if a person had to reach it fast.

Six weeks ago he had been trading two hours of hauling for a heel of bread and reckoning whether it was enough to split.

*Does this keep her safe,* he thought, and the answer was yes, and so he adjusted the strap on his shoulder and kept walking.

—

She was waiting at the far gate, and she had not been there a moment ago.

Dusty was reasonably certain of that. He had swept the gate twice on the approach the way he swept every doorway he had ever walked through, and the archway had held nothing but stone and two guards and a shadow. Now there was a woman standing in the center of it with her hands folded at her waist, as though she had been standing there since the bridge was built and everyone else had simply failed to notice.

Black dress, high at the throat. White apron, so clean it hurt to look at in the morning light. Skirt cut just above the knee over white stockings, black lace gloves to the wrist, and heels that should not have been able to stand on cobblestone without sinking. Her figure filled the uniform the way a sword fills a scabbard — precisely, and with the strong impression that it had not been made for decoration. Dark hair pinned up without a strand loose. Red on her mouth, applied with the exactness of a signature.

She was smiling, and the smile reached her eyes, and Dusty did not trust it for a second — not because it was false. Because it wasn't.

"Dusty Desert." Not a question. "Welcome to Mythrandis. I am Mirelle. I will be showing you the island today."

"Thank you, ma'am." He inclined his head. "I was told someone would meet me. I thought it would be Researcher Velanthus."

"Researcher Velanthus intended to." Something moved at the corner of Mirelle's mouth, brief as a wingbeat. "She is currently in the east lab. She has been in the east lab since the day before yesterday. Someone will feed her at noon."

"That sounds right."

"It usually is." Mirelle turned, and her hand came out and took the satchel off his shoulder before he had finished deciding whether to let her. "This will be in your room before you are."

Behind them, the crimson doublet arrived at the gate at speed.

"You." The young lord's finger came up. "My trunks are being manhandled by dockworkers. See that they're brought to my—"

Mirelle turned to him and the smile did not change at all.

There were two trunks coming up the road behind him on a low cart, brass-cornered, big as coffins, with three porters walking beside them and looking unhappy about the grade of the hill. Mirelle stepped off the path. She reached down, took the strap handle of the nearest trunk in one lace-gloved hand, and lifted it off the cart.

She did not brace. She did not shift her weight. The heels did not sink.

She set it down on the cobbles beside the young lord's boots with a sound like a dropped stone, straightened, and folded her hands again at her waist.

"All actions must be with Beauty, Elegance, and Dignity above all else," she said pleasantly. "Yours will be brought up, my lord. There is no need to raise your voice on a bridge. Sound carries."

The silence that followed had a texture to it.

Dusty watched the young lord's face work through three separate expressions and land, at last, on the one belonging to a person who does not understand what has just happened but has correctly identified that something has. He made a sound in his throat. He went up the road.

The porters looked at Mirelle. Mirelle looked back at them. They found somewhere else to look.

Only Dusty had been watching her forearm when she lifted, and only Dusty had noticed that the lace across it had not tightened. Not by a thread. Whatever that trunk weighed, her arm had not been informed.

"Shall we?" said Mirelle.

"Yes, ma'am," said Dusty, and followed.

—

She walked him east through a morning that kept refusing to be believable.

The bridge district first — guard quarters, workhouses, the long low buildings where the island's business got done out of sight of the students it was done for. Then the road opened, and the road was lined with trees, and the trees had been planted by someone who had known exactly how tall they would be in a hundred years.

"North," Mirelle said, "is where you will sleep. The dormitories. Single rooms, all of them, and assignments are random — you will hear otherwise from students who cannot accept that a thing was not arranged in their favor. It was not arranged at all." A pause. "By anyone you will ever meet."

"Understood."

"Central is where you will spend most of your waking hours. Lecture halls, the libraries, the research wings. The towers hold the administration, and the Headmaster at the top of them. You will very likely never see him. If you do see him, something has gone extremely well or extremely badly, and you will know which."

Marble statues stood in the courtyards. Fountains ran in the middle of them, and nobody was drinking from them, and nobody was watching to see if anyone did. Students crossed the paths in twos and threes — colored blazers, black ties, skirts and stockings and boots polished to a shine, girls with ribbons at their collars laughing with their heads tipped back, a boy carrying six books that were plainly not his while their owner walked ahead of him and did not look back once.

Dusty catalogued it without effort. Who walked in front. Who walked half a step behind and to the left. Who laughed a beat too late at the joke.

Nothing here was new. Only the fabric was better.

"Dining halls, seven of them, all through the central district. Breakfast at seven, lunch at twelve, dinner at six." Mirelle's heels struck the stone in a rhythm as steady as a metronome. "Tea is at four. Tea is not optional in the way you are about to assume it is optional."

"No, ma'am."

"Curfew is at ten. The Guard hold the island's edges and the catacomb entrances. There are a great many doors on this island that are locked, and every one of them is locked for a reason, and the reasons are older than your family name." She said it as lightly as she had said everything else. "Students test this. It is traditional. I would rather you did not."

"I have no plans to."

"No." Mirelle glanced at him, once, and it was not a long look, but it went all the way down. "No, I don't imagine you do."

East, she took him past a wall of glass. It ran the length of a building longer than any street he had lived on, and behind it the air was visibly warmer — greenery pressed against the panes, broad leaves and climbing vines and rows of low herb beds running back into a golden haze. Beyond the glass, figures in white moved between beds. A woman knelt beside a boy with a splinted arm, and her hands glowed a soft yellow, and the boy's shoulders came down out of his ears.

Dusty stopped walking.

He was not aware of having decided to.

"The Solarium," said Mirelle, who had stopped when he did. "Infirmary and growing houses both. You will be rotated through it in your second term, and after that, if you are any good, they will stop rotating you and simply expect you." She let that sit. "Inside those doors there is no rank. None. Not house, not blood, not lottery. A duke's daughter and a fisherman's son bleed on adjacent tables and are treated in the order they arrived, and any student who forgets that is removed from the building by us. Not by the Guard. By us."

"Who enforces it when the Maids aren't there?"

"There is no such condition," said Mirelle.

He looked at her. She looked back, entirely pleasant.

"Thank you," Dusty said. "That's the first thing anyone's told me today that I can use."

Something in her face shifted — nothing so crude as surprise. A recalibration, small and internal, filed somewhere he would never see.

"South," she went on, as they turned, "is faculty housing, and the Maid Corp quarters, and the docks. The docks are not for students. The forest along the southern shore is not forbidden, but it is not recommended, and I will tell you plainly that the distinction matters less than you would like it to."

"May I ask you one thing?"

"You may ask me a great many things. I answer some of them."

"Where does the mail go out from?"

Mirelle stopped.

It was the only time all morning her rhythm broke, and it lasted less than a second, and if he had not been raised where he was raised he would not have caught it at all.

"The correspondence office. Central district, western face, second floor." Her head tilted very slightly. "Everything in and out is read. Every letter, without exception, including the ones from emperors. I tell every student this on the first day and most of them are outraged."

"I'm not."

"No." The red mouth curved. "You have nothing to hide and someone to write to. That is a rarer combination here than you would expect. Second floor, western face. They open at eight."

—

Tea happened at four, and he watched it from the edge of a courtyard the way he had once watched a market — reading the flow, learning where the money moved.

He had been given his blazer by then. Dark green, stiff with newness, and it fit him well enough across the shoulders that he understood someone had measured him without his noticing. Under the tea pavilions, maids in black and white moved among the tables pouring from silver pots, and at one table a girl with a white shawl across her shoulders said something that made nine people laugh at once, and at another table three lower-born students sat very straight and did not touch their cups until someone else did.

Nobody spoke to him. Two people looked at his tie, then at his boots, and adjusted their route.

That suited him precisely. He had never once wanted anything from a room except to be unremarkable in it.

—

His room was on the third floor of the northern dormitory, second-to-last door, and it was two rooms, which he had not expected.

A washroom with a copper tub and running water that came out warm when he turned the tap, which he did twice to be certain. Then the main room: a desk. A chair. A wardrobe. A bed with four pillows, when one would have been generous and none was what he was used to.

His satchel sat on the desk exactly as promised. He had not passed a single maid on the stairs.

Dusty set his palm against the door and pushed it closed, and stood a while in the dark with his back to it.

Then he worked.

Window first — it opened, and it opened onto a ledge, and the ledge ran twelve feet to a downspout that would hold his weight for about four seconds, which was three more than he would need. Drop to the garden below: twenty feet to grass. Survivable. The wardrobe was deep enough to stand in. The desk could be dragged against the door in under two seconds if he moved it eight inches to the left now, so he moved it eight inches to the left now. The corridor floor outside had a board that spoke, four paces from his threshold.

None of it was paranoia. It was the same as checking a knot before you trusted it with your weight. The body did not unlearn survival because the sheets were clean.

When it was done he sat on the edge of the bed in a dark green blazer worth more than his mother's funeral and took the waxed paper packet out of the satchel.

Inside was a flower. Pressed flat, dried to translucence, a small five-petaled thing the pale blue of a winter sky. It had grown in a clay pot on the sill of a healer's house in Aethres, and it had a name, because Rinnie named all of them, and she had never once told him this one's.

He held it in the flat of his hand for a while.

Then, because it was habit and because the room was very quiet, he let the mana come.

It rose the way it always had — no rush of it, no flood, just the small steady wick of it catching somewhere under his sternum and running down his arms into his fingers. The glow bloomed across both palms. Black light, which had made three separate hedge-healers in the Greys refuse to touch him, outlined in a thread of moving silver, and it lit nothing at all. Black light does not illuminate. It only shows you where the hands are.

He warmed the air over the flower. Not the flower — you did not put mana into a dead thing, it went straight through and came out the other side. Just the air.

For eleven winters he had done exactly this, all night, every night, in rooms with no glass in the windows. Small fire. No well to drain. He had never once been the strongest thing in any room he had ever stood in, and it had never mattered, because he had never needed to be strongest.

He had only ever needed to not stop.

There was nobody in this room to keep warm.

Dusty sat with his hands glowing black and silver in the dark for a long moment, and then he closed them, and the light went out, and he put the flower on the desk against the base of the lamp where he would see it when he woke.

He lay down on top of the blankets with his boots on and his back to the wall, facing the door.

Outside, somewhere over the lake, the towers rang the hour.

He was asleep before the third bell, and he slept the way he always slept — lightly, one layer down, the part of him that counted exits still awake and still counting.
